frr10: two fixes, one per paragraph below.

1. zebra ignores the neighbor cache entries the FreeBSD kernel reports
on the routing socket.

When an IPv6 neighbor entry resolves, nd6.c sends RTM_ADD with
RTF_HOST | RTF_LLDATA (and RTM_DELETE when it expires). zebra's
rtm_read() skips RTF_WASCLONED but not RTF_LLDATA, so every IPv6
neighbor on a connected network becomes a "kernel" /128 route at
distance 0 ("K>* 2001:db8:113::10/128 is directly connected"), which
overrides static and BGP routes to the same host: in the rtbh lab, the
IPv6 blackhole trigger (a static /128 tagged 666) is never selected
("over-ridden by better route").

2. RPKI revalidation never reaches the routes

When the RPKI cache adds or withdraws a ROA, bgpd revalidates the routes
it covers: rpki_revalidate_prefix() looks the ROA prefix up with
bgp_table_subtree_lookup(), then re-runs the inbound policy on every
route of that subtree. bgp_table_subtree_lookup() walks the trie through
bgp_dests, but the trie's internal nodes have none: it returns NULL as
soon as it meets one, and the top node is internal whenever the table
holds two prefixes without a common covering route. The routes then keep
their old state until a manual "clear bgp ... soft in": in the rpki lab,
a hijack that a new ROA makes invalid stays the best route ("I*>").

Walk the route nodes instead, and return the subtree root as a locked
route_node, which may be an internal node: rpki_revalidate_prefix()
walks the subtree with route_next_until() and skips the nodes without a
bgp_dest. Nothing is allocated, and the lock is released by the walk.
